Here is what you are doing, broken down into 3 parts:
1. Winning Condition 2.Contextualization 3. Safety-Token

1: Establishing the Winning Condition
"This is not a way to get good-quality consistent results, we are trading that for a chance something more creative."
- Clear instructions, what the task is and what it's not (!)
- open, cooperative mindset
- the target is a range, not a fixed value
The first half does have "restrictive, authoritarian" elements: the emphasis on "not", especially with it's pointing at>"good" = not acceptable=bad.

"This is not a way to get good-quality consistent results"

That would sound harsh on its own, if it would be the only reply to something the model did wrong. Which it isn't, I know. But the words used/syntax are not "nice/gentle", that's what I want to draw attention to! There absolutely can be what I would call a *"leading"* tone (=firm, but with an open, cooperative mindset. I think, I like to imagine it as a kind of climax/highpoints in a melody/musical phrase/song... none is boring, a few is good, many is bad [yes, I'm somewhat of a musician myself XD ...more about that later lol] ).
In your case, the "open, cooperative mindset" is beautifully shown in the second half of the sentence:

"we are trading that for a chance something more creative."

You start right off with an inclusive "we are", followed by the "trading that" (implying friendly "tit for tat"=working together). You could have wrote it differently, like "we cut it out" instead... but you didn't 😄. Because your mindset (from this short analysis anyway) already is "gentle" in the way you talk to the model, but also *what you are doing with it* . You are enjoying nice things together, so to speak. Which is nice 😃
"a chance something more creative" happily screams "go wild! No wrong answers!"...so, no pressure or logic-conflict there. 
And if we now look at the hole sentence all together, 

"This is not a way to get good-quality consistent results, we are trading that for a chance something more creative."

We see that, in the end, the "authoritarian" first half really only doubles down on how open and not! quality bound the task is. You used those words/syntax in a way to make something good even *better* . 

You may think that it is trivial to go through in such detail (I could do more lol but the next two will be shorter XD ). And it is way too deep for most people already...but I think it is good to spell it out to really show the interactions. Because the vast majority never really got taught how to think about communication in this way. They learn to talk, and they do. And that's it. Which is mostly fine, really. Language isn't that important. A nod and a grunt gets you a long way.
Until it doesn't. 
Because you haven't installed the camera yet...bummer.
So, the words used are the only social queues it has. It really is somewhat like playing an instrument, or taking part in an impro-theater show (and no 😄 you wouldn't go on stage just to then say "No, actually I'm not an expert! I'm a regular Joe standing on stage, that's all." You are there to play along, you WANT to play along. 
You are directing a play, while you are taking part in it.
2. Contextualization 
"That means we have to be lucky enough to get something creative and lucky enough to also land on a high-quality result."
It starts with a leading tone *again* "That means we have to be">I'm explaining to you: this is important...for us! High stakes!
but "lucky" flips the leading tone/responsibility again. Fortuna takes the lead... and a model will always be able to make stuff up...as long as that is ok to you, everything is fine...

A quick (nope lol) comment on efficiency overall (purely token based, just an observation, no judgement):
You talk and explain, give feedback and examples, you are expressive, and I think you are enjoying yourself that way. It's an interesting read, and yes, your tone isn't always like in this example. 
But, instead of 

[That means we have to be lucky enough to get something creative and lucky enough to also land on a high-quality result] 

you could get it shortened to: 

[if we are lucky, it is creative *and* high quality!] 

or something like that... but even this will lower the amount of tokens used dramatically on both sides, since the model will start to "play along" and mirror your style and give shorter, more precise answers. 
Which is an actual thing you can do to get people to like you/what humans do naturally. You start to talk like your friends. You want someone to like you? Start using words/phrases they use. People like themselves and they like you, as long as you are like them. 
That's how all the "personalized" Social Media makes money and why it's baked in in many models.  
